 :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-27538/selection/0f7278317c0acb888a32eaa3af7f9986b651a34ba7f7c3a5b7ebfa6e46e2a86c" :answer-field/choices ["q-27538/selection/70f32dc6255dae256949ca8efbf30753f79dd0a86285e6220c2b1c9074192c35" "q-27538/selection/0f7278317c0acb888a32eaa3af7f9986b651a34ba7f7c3a5b7ebfa6e46e2a86c" "q-27538/selection/77b2e90b49a6d63c7e7a0c4936b303473935d4822e652f575b4ddfb04b1a59f7" "q-27538/selection/9d7344fb77fa3d9372958b77c056c055e342cbddf4b0743668276a624b15dd5f" "q-27538/selection/160a4cdd54cee8335701c5faf3d0b089fca54f3e946e5ca9d21a73d0e832711b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-27538" :question/id #uuid "b5e7ffcf-d49d-5dfe-9c73-ca9c0369ba7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-27538" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y=3{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}-1?$" :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941829/assets/4cdc9e4fa78513bca01fa10242ba6c3f12b31c262448a8edf350c76e84b188b8.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=3{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $3.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,3)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941829/assets/8e7d6c5eb931f55a9f94ac33382c0e1ec9e9e49f50b3ab538d9848d6a77be665.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=3{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}-1.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,3)$ to $(0,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941829/assets/9d332fa8d3d8ef65daaf34bf906c5ca7cd7e2edaf562386ed4e0ebc4555b0a64.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-27538/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-27538"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-99724/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "fa12f1ad-c232-531e-9ced-e67ec2ccc37e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99724/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "7f52c9bc-532a-5dfb-83b3-32d8080e998a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99724/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "d6fdcc9c-ea41-5ffe-b910-f0f2ad4b2393" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99724/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "984e5e7c-0703-5c7e-90ce-8bd42ddedd8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99724/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "3ab06f8f-7e41-5ffa-b015-1370cc8c8216" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-99724/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f6cfc28-d5dc-5fc5-b52d-5fa3f05f787e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-99724/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-99724/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-99724/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-99724/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-99724/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-99724/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-99724" :question/id #uuid "87942db4-38db-57bf-9c17-cbe92961b339" :question/math-academy-id "q-99724" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941829/assets/cdce64bf5d02850bbedc2b89af3b2e7447b733831c09d1955e4a9ee97e82d556.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$ Hence, $c=2.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,4),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+2 & =4 \\\\ k+2 & =4 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-99724/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99724"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-170351/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "b4a5bc42-fa40-5853-973e-a26670caec4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170351/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "05e5ffae-43fd-5c80-8727-b694451434f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170351/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" :answer/id #uuid "907f9f5d-a0d9-5821-95ac-0ca8bc6916f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170351/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "76746881-edec-59f2-8647-624e9234b4bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170351/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "a9ec88d3-6607-5609-99bc-087423649d0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170351/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8c7224-6d03-51f6-8c21-7d3b7b8e4a46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170351/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-170351/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-170351/selection/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" "q-170351/selection/e53026d2e72b5a9665afb169ba986753fff36dfc4557e460320cb8d9594cece8" "q-170351/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-170351/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170351" :question/id #uuid "121d554e-5f4e-5371-8473-8b1847a35280" :question/math-academy-id "q-170351" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941829/assets/bc0e258229c74a97d61919440090dd7b2f0f4a200af987477d2be8818910a88b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{1}{4},$ hence $c=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{4} & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k+\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ k & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\frac{5}{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}+\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-170351/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170351"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
